  <meta name="description" content="What {{ metadata.row_count }} engaged Feral players like, dislike, and want changed about the currently revealed Forever design.">
  <title>Forever Feral Community Feedback</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="report.css">
</head>
<body>
<header class="topbar">
  <a class="brand" href="../"><span class="crest" aria-hidden="true">❦</span><span><small>WORLD OF WARCRAFT</small><strong>FOREVER</strong></span></a>
  <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../">Talents</a><a href="../abilities.html">Abilities</a><a class="active" href="./" aria-current="page">Feral survey</a></nav>
  <a class="method-link" href="methodology.html">Methods &amp; downloads</a>
</header>
<main>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">COMMUNITY FEEDBACK · {{ metadata.row_count }} RESPONSES</p>
    <h1>The clearest Feral feedback is about the <em>play loop</em>—not one required mechanic.</h1>
    <p class="deck">This report separates the argument over literal powershifting from the broader design question: how should Feral preserve activity, resource decisions, mastery, and a meaningful reason to use more than one form?</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="#story">Read the story</a><a class="ghost-button" href="methodology.html">See the evidence</a></div>
  </section>

  <section id="story" class="story-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
    <h2>Three results tell the story.</h2>
    <div class="story-grid">
      <article class="story-card"><span>01</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Negative toward current direction"]) }}%</strong><h3>Current sentiment is negative.</h3><p>A majority feels negative about the current direction, and {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Less excited than Classic"]) }}% feel less excited than they do about Classic Feral.</p></article>
      <article class="story-card"><span>02</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Do not require literal powershifting"]) }}%</strong><h3>Most do not require literal powershifting.</h3><p>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Functional replacement acceptable"]) }}% would accept a functional replacement, while {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Prefer non-powershifting if engaging"]) }}% actively prefer an engaging non-powershifting design.</p></article>
      <article class="story-card"><span>03</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Negative reaction to Furor/loss of powershifting"]) }}%</strong><h3>The proposed replacement has not resolved the concern.</h3><p>Openness to a new solution should not be mistaken for approval of the revealed Furor and energy model.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="thesis"><p>The clearest reading is not “everyone wants Classic powershifting back.” It is: <strong>if powershifting goes away, the replacement still has to make Feral active, thoughtful, expressive, and recognizably Druid.</strong></p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="origin panel" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">PROJECT INTENT</p><h2 id="origin-title">Turn a heated design argument into feedback developers can use.</h2></div>
    <div class="origin-copy">
      <p>I’m <strong>JVMES</strong> in the Druid Classic Discord. I started this project after watching the Forever reveal discussion collapse several different questions into a single yes-or-no fight over powershifting. My concern was broader: removing powershifting could also remove the activity, resource conversion, and form-based decisions that made Feral rewarding unless a deliberate replacement took their place.</p>
      <p>I did not create the survey to prove that Classic powershifting must return. It was designed to test whether that concern was shared, distinguish attachment to a mechanic from attachment to the experience it produced, and keep separate issues—bleeds, hybrid play, AoE, utility, scaling, and raid value—from being treated as one complaint.</p>
      <p>The intended output is a practical brief for Blizzard’s developers: not a petition for one implementation, but a map of where respondents are flexible, what they consistently prioritize, what the reveal already gets right, and which problems still need a direct design answer.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="context-note">Exports from <strong>#forever-general</strong>, <strong>#forever-feral-dps</strong>, and <strong>#forever-feral-tank</strong>, along with a <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/classicwow/comments/1wljuy6/">public r/classicwow survey announcement</a>, document why and how the project began. They provide context only. They were not coded, counted, quoted, or included in the analysis; the survey responses are the evidence used below.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">HOW THE REVEAL LANDED</p><h2>Broad sentiment is negative, even though many people would still try the spec.</h2><p>Interest in playing Feral is more resilient than excitement about the design. That gives developers room to win people back—but not a reason to dismiss the criticism.</p></div>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <figure><img src="figures/overall-direction.svg" alt="Bar chart comparing reactions to the current Forever Feral direction."><figcaption>Overall direction · all {{ metadata.row_count }} responses</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="figures/excitement.svg" alt="Bar chart comparing excitement with Classic Feral."><figcaption>Excitement relative to Classic · all {{ metadata.row_count }} responses</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <figure class="wide-chart"><img src="figures/likelihood-to-play.svg" alt="Bar chart showing likelihood to main or regularly play Feral."><figcaption>Would play: {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Would probably/definitely play"]) }}% · Unsure and not-applicable answers remain visible in the full data.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="signal-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">WHERE THE SIGNAL IS CONCENTRATED</p><h2>This is primarily a Cat and hybrid-play concern.</h2><p>The overall number should not be read as uniform rejection of every Feral role. Primary interest and direct test-build exposure materially change the result.</p></div>
    <div class="signal-grid">
      <article><h3>Negative reaction by primary interest</h3><div class="signal-row"><span>Cat DPS</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Cat DPS interest"]["Negative direction"]) }}%</strong></div><div class="signal-row"><span>Hybrid / off-tank</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Hybrid/off-tank interest"]["Negative direction"]) }}%</strong></div><div class="signal-row"><span>Bear tank</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Bear tank interest"]["Negative direction"]) }}%</strong></div><p>Bear-focused respondents evaluate the revealed direction much more favorably than Cat- and hybrid-focused respondents.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Negative reaction by familiarity</h3><div class="signal-row"><span>Played the test build</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Played test build"]["Negative direction"]) }}%</strong></div><div class="signal-row"><span>Reviewed / discussed only</span><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Reviewed/discussed only"]["Negative direction"]) }}%</strong></div><p>Direct players are less negative than respondents evaluating from detailed public information, but a majority of test-build players remain negative.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="context-note">These are descriptive comparisons inside a self-selected sample. Primary-interest and familiarity groups answer different questions and should not be compared as if they were one partition.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="split-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">THE POWERSHIFTING QUESTION</p><h2>Most respondents are flexible about the mechanic, but consistent about the desired experience.</h2><p>Only {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Specifically want Classic powershifting restored"]) }}% chose literal Classic-style restoration as their closest view. Most respondents leave room for another answer. The importance, agreement, priority, and written-response questions independently reinforce the demand for activity, resource decisions, and mastery.</p></div>
    <figure><img src="figures/powershifting-preference.svg" alt="Bar chart comparing six views of powershifting and possible replacements."><figcaption>One choice per respondent. “Not enough information” is retained.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="mechanics-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT TO KEEP</p><h2>There are real wins in the revealed direction.</h2><p>The criticism is not a rejection of everything new. Several individual ideas are well received and give the design a foundation to build on.</p></div>
    <div class="keep-grid">
      <article><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(mechanic_summary["Clearcasting / Omen of Clarity"]) }}%</strong><span>positive on Clearcasting / Omen</span></article>
      <article><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(mechanic_summary["Tiger's Fury"]) }}%</strong><span>positive on Tiger’s Fury</span></article>
      <article><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(mechanic_summary["Rip/Rake and the bleed emphasis"]) }}%</strong><span>positive on the bleed emphasis</span></article>
    </div>
    <figure class="wide-chart"><img src="figures/mechanic-reactions.svg" alt="Two-sided chart comparing positive and negative reactions to individual mechanics."><figcaption>Positive and negative reactions are shown side by side; neutral and not-enough-information answers remain in the downloadable table.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="priority-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT NEEDS WORK</p><h2>Fix the play loop first. Treat the other concerns as their own problems.</h2><p>Rotation and activity dominate the requested attention. Energy and powershifting are closely related, but AoE, hybrid play, talents, and raid value should not be reduced to the same argument.</p></div>
    <figure><img src="figures/developer-attention.svg" alt="Bar chart of the most-selected areas needing developer attention."><figcaption>Respondents could select up to three areas, so these percentages are not meant to add to 100%.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="recommendations">
      <article><b>1</b><div><h3>Give Cat a reliable activity engine.</h3><p>Test powershifting, faster or resettable Tiger’s Fury, richer Clearcasting interactions, bleed-driven resource gains, and other resource-conversion ideas against the same goal: less empty time with meaningful choices.</p></div></article>
      <article><b>2</b><div><h3>Make shifting feel useful, not merely thematic.</h3><p>Cat/Bear/caster weaving and hybrid off-tanking matter independently of literal powershifting. Form changes should create decisions rather than punish the player.</p></div></article>
      <article><b>3</b><div><h3>Keep the good ideas, then make them active.</h3><p>Clearcasting, Tiger’s Fury, and bleed identity are strong starting points. The bleed direction needs interaction and payoff, not only passive maintenance.</p></div></article>
      <article><b>4</b><div><h3>Handle AoE, utility, and scaling directly.</h3><p>Swipe scaling, dungeon usefulness, raid desirability, tanking, and numerical tuning are not automatically solved by changing the Cat rotation.</p></div></article>
    </div>
    <div class="brief-table" aria-label="Developer design brief">
      <div class="brief-head"><span>Observed signal</span><span>Design objective</span><span>Candidate levers respondents would test</span><span>Risk to validate</span></div>
      <div class="brief-row"><p><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(importance_summary["Meaningful energy and resource decisions"]) }}%</strong> rate resource decisions highly; <strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(priority_summary["Rotation/activity level"]) }}%</strong> prioritized activity; <strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(negative_mechanics["Energy generation and pooling"]) }}%</strong> react negatively to the energy model.</p><p>Create meaningful resource decisions without excessive empty time.</p><p>Tiger’s Fury changes ({{ "%.1f"|format(direction_summary["Use Tiger's Fury more frequently or allow gameplay-driven cooldown resets"]) }}%), resource conversion ({{ "%.1f"|format(direction_summary["Add another active resource-conversion mechanic"]) }}%), or Clearcasting interactions ({{ "%.1f"|format(direction_summary["Add more Clearcasting interactions"]) }}%).</p><p>Excessive APM, mandatory complexity, or making energy irrelevant.</p></div>
      <div class="brief-row"><p><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(disagreement_summary["The current design preserves Feral's shapeshifting identity."]) }}%</strong> disagree that the current design preserves shapeshifting identity.</p><p>Make leaving Cat situationally valuable rather than automatically punitive.</p><p>Intentional Cat/Bear/caster incentives ({{ "%.1f"|format(direction_summary["Add intentional Cat/Bear/caster weaving incentives"]) }}%).</p><p>A mandatory rotational tax, role confusion, or balance exploits.</p></div>
      <div class="brief-row"><p><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(mechanic_summary["Rip/Rake and the bleed emphasis"]) }}%</strong> react positively to bleed emphasis.</p><p>Turn the accepted theme into active decisions and payoff.</p><p>Bleed-driven energy or active interactions ({{ "%.1f"|format(direction_summary["Let bleeds interact with energy or other active decisions"]) }}%).</p><p>Bleed immunity, ramp time, target switching, and encounter variance.</p></div>
      <div class="brief-row"><p><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(negative_mechanics["AoE / Swipe tools"]) }}%</strong> react negatively to AoE / Swipe.</p><p>Give Cat and Bear dependable value in routine multi-target play.</p><p>Swipe scaling, toolkit coverage, and tuning independent of the single-target loop.</p><p>Three-target constraints, threat balance, burst windows, and crowd-control expectations.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="context-note">Candidate levers are options respondents said they would be open to testing, not votes for a final implementation. The survey identifies design constraints; playtests and telemetry must determine the solution.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="language-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">RECURRING LANGUAGE</p><h2>The written answers describe the experience respondents want to preserve.</h2><p>Across the three open questions, recurring phrases connect the same ideas in different ways: activity, energy, skill expression, form use, weapon scaling, bleeds, and specific alternatives to the revealed Furor model.</p></div>
    <div class="cloud-grid">{% for q, label in [(14, "What developers should understand"), (15, "What should be preserved"), (16, "One requested change")] %}<figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q{{ q }}.png" alt="Phrase cloud for {{ label|lower }}, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q{{ q }} · {{ label }}</figcaption><ol class="phrase-list">{% for item in phrase_top[q] %}<li><span>{{ item.term }}</span><strong>{{ item.response_mentions }}/{{ item.responses_with_text }}</strong></li>{% endfor %}</ol></figure>{% endfor %}</div>
    <p class="context-note">Cloud sizes are relative within each question. The ranked counts show how many distinct responses contained each phrase; even the largest phrases occur in a minority of answers. These are language patterns, not separate survey votes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="profile-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">SAMPLE COMPOSITION</p><h2>The report most clearly represents highly engaged organized-PvE players.</h2><p>{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * sample_counts.experience["High-end or competitive Feral player"] / metadata.row_count) }}% selected “high-end or competitive,” while {{ "%.1f"|format(100 * sample_counts.experience["Regular organized-raiding Feral player"] / metadata.row_count) }}% selected “regular organized-raiding.” That concentration defines whose priorities this report captures most clearly.</p></div>
    <div class="profile-grid">
      <figure><img src="figures/experience-profile.svg" alt="Bar chart of respondents' self-described Feral experience levels."><figcaption>One self-selected experience description per respondent.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="profile-note"><h3>“Competitive” is a self-report, not a verified rating.</h3><p>The survey did not collect combat logs, parses, rankings, progression, account history, or any other external measure of performance. The category indicates how respondents identify their own relationship to Feral; it does not establish their actual competitive level.</p><p>Within this sample, the self-described high-end/competitive group was more negative about the current direction ({{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["High-end/competitive"]["Negative direction"]) }}%) than regular organized raiders ({{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Regular raiding"]["Negative direction"]) }}%) or the combined casual/newer/other group ({{ "%.1f"|format(subgroup_summary["Casual/newer/other"]["Negative direction"]) }}%). That is a descriptive association, not evidence that competitive experience caused the difference.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="voices-section">
    <div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">IN THEIR OWN WORDS</p><h2>Different problems and solutions—not one monolithic demand.</h2><p>These full excerpts were deliberately selected to represent contrasting design concerns. They were manually reviewed, stripped of accidental identifiers, and come only from respondents who allowed anonymous quotation.</p></div>
    <div class="quote-grid">{% for _, row in featured_quotes.iterrows() %}<blockquote><span class="quote-label">{{ row.viewpoint }}</span><p>“{{ row.excerpt }}”</p><footer>{{ row.question }} · quoted with permission</footer></blockquote>{% endfor %}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="sample-section panel">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">WHO ANSWERED</p><h2>An engaged Feral audience—not the entire player base.</h2></div>
    <p>This report covers <strong>{{ metadata.row_count }} valid responses</strong> collected from {{ collection_start }} through {{ collection_end }}. {{ sample_counts.familiarity["I have played the current test build"] }} respondents played the test build, while {{ sample_counts.familiarity["I have closely reviewed talents, abilities, footage, or theorycrafting"] + sample_counts.familiarity["I have followed detailed community discussion"] }} closely reviewed or discussed it.</p>
    <p class="context-note">Recruitment through community channels produced a rapid, self-selected response. That makes the survey useful for understanding these engaged respondents’ priorities, but not representative of every potential Forever player.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">BOTTOM LINE</p><h2>Do not confuse flexibility about the solution with satisfaction about the problem.</h2><p>These respondents give the design team room to move beyond Classic powershifting. Their consistent expectation is that Cat Feral should stay active, reward mastery, make resources matter, and retain meaningful reasons to shapeshift.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="methodology.html">Methods, survey instrument &amp; downloads</a></div></section>
</main>
<footer class="page-foot"><span>Forever Feral community feedback · live snapshot retrieved {{ retrieved_display }}</span><a href="../">Return to the Druid calculator</a></footer>
</body>
</html>
